## How Generics Compile

### Source Code
```xiom
fn first[T](items: &Vec[T]) -> T {
  return items[0];
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let nums = Vec[Int](); nums.push(42);
  let strs = Vec[Str](); strs.push("hello");
  let a = first(&nums);  // T = Int
  let b = first(&strs);  // T = Str
  return a;
}
```

### After Monomorphisation (Conceptual)
```xiom
fn first_Int(items: &Vec[Int]) -> Int {
  return items[0];
}

fn first_Str(items: &Vec[Str]) -> Str {
  return items[0];
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let nums = Vec[Int](); nums.push(42);
  let strs = Vec[Str](); strs.push("hello");
  let a = first_Int(&nums);
  let b = first_Str(&strs);
  return a;
}
```

The compiler generates a SEPARATE copy of `first` for each concrete type used — Int, Str, Bool, etc.

### Benefits
- **Zero runtime overhead**: No vtables, no dynamic dispatch, no boxing
- **Inlining possible**: The compiler can inline the concrete version
- **Type-specific optimizations**: Different code paths for different types

### Costs
- **Larger binary**: One copy per type (but usually negligible)
- **Longer compile times**: More code to generate and optimize

### Monomorphisation vs Dynamic Dispatch
| Feature | Monomorphisation | Dynamic Dispatch |
|---------|-----------------|------------------|
| Speed | Fast (direct call) | Slower (vtable lookup) |
| Binary size | Larger (per type) | Smaller (one version) |
| Inlining | Yes | Difficult |

